			  OS/8 <-> VMS/RSX HANDLER

     The VAX:/RSX: handlers allow PDP8 programmers using the OS/8 (OS/78)
operating system to send files to, or receive files from, other large DEC
computer systems using VMS, RSX or TOPS10/20 operating systems.  All com-
munication is through the host system's terminal driver, hence no special
transfer programs are required on the remote machine.  In fact, the files
may include operating system commands, making it possible to initiate pro-
cessing on the large machine which then runs in parallel with programs on
the PDP8.

     The basic handler is available in three formats: one for the VAX, one
for '11 systems, and one for '10 systems.  They have the names: VAX:, RSX:
and TEN:, respectively.  For purposes of illustration only the VAX: handler
will be discussed below, however operations with the RSX: or TEN: handlers
are entirely similar, with only minor differences.

     Note that since the handlers may be used with any OS/8 program, the re-
sulting 'network' is significantly more versatile than one which uses a spe-
cial 'file-transfer' program on either end.  The only restriction is that
OS/8 FORTRAN IV programs cannot use this handler for INPUT at the same time
that they use any other non-system handler for I/O operations.  This is due
to a design deficiency in FRTS that applies to all handlers that buffer data
within themselves.  There are no restrictions on OUTPUT.

     The handler may be installed either by using the BUILD program (see the
OS/8 Handbook, Ch. 2), or by using the OS/78 SET HANDLER command.  After the
hander is installed, the user may run the VAXX (or RSXX or TENX) program to
change various characteristics, for instance the device codes used, the 'con-
catenation switch' or the 'delay-time' setting; the use of the VAXX program
is described separately.  It is also necessary to have a 'terminal emulator'
program available that will allow you to connect the OS/8 console to the re-
mote machine.  This program is usually called TERMIN so it may be invoked by
the '.TER' command.  The version of TERMIN supplied with the handler is also
described separately.

     The VAX: handler has been designed to interface with the standard VMS
terminal driver.  However, the following terminal characteristics should be
set before you attempt to use the handler: SET TERM/TAB/FORM/WIDTH=132/LOWER
/TTSYNC/HOSTSYNC.  Of these, the last two are the most important since they
control the synchronization required between the two machines.  'HOSTSYNC'
means that the VAX will respond to XON/XOFF signals sent from the '8, while
'TTSYNC' means that the VAX will send similar signals back to the '8.  This
command (or something equivalent) can be included in your LOGIN.COM file, or
it can be placed in the system startup file.  (Note that the 'TTSYNC' option
has only recently been implemented under RSX; the handler will thus NOT WORK
with older versions of that operating system.)


			    Sending files to the VAX

     To send files to the VAX one must first go through the usual LOGIN pro-
cedure and then issue a 'CREATE' command to copy data into a VMS file:

		.R TERMIN		(or use .TER with OS/78)
		Username: ...
		Password: ...
		$ CREATE 'filespec'	(hit 'return', then Ctrl/E)
     On other systems 'CREATE' may be replaced by a 'COPY filespec=TTY:' com-
mand (the ordering of input/output may differ).  This command should be termi-
nated by hitting the RETURN key, at which point any additional data sent will
be put in the file.  The next step is thus to exit from the TERMIN program by
typing 'Ctrl/E' and return to the OS/8 monitor:

		(Type Ctrl/E to exit from the TERMIN program)
		.COPY VAX:<filename	(this uses the FOTP program)
		FILES COPIED:
		filename		(only one at a time for now)
		.R TERMIN
		$ DIR filespec		(check that the file exists)
		$ TYPE filespec		(look at some of it)
		$ PRINT/delete filespec	(print it out and delete it)
		$ LOGOUT		(or whatever...)
		(Type Ctrl/E to return to OS/8)

     Aside from the login procedure which is best performed interactively so
you can respond to mail, note system down-times, etc., the commands which
create the file can just as well be sent via the handler itself.  Thus after
logging in, one can immediately go back to OS/8 to run a program which sends
the appropriate CREATE command (plus a Carriage Return), followed by the ac-
tual data.  This allows 'unattended' operation which may even be done by a
BATCH procedure, which is extremely convenient when processing large amounts
of data.

     Note that unlike almost all other 'non-file-structured' handlers, the
VAX: handler normally sends the 'CTRL/Z' which OS/8 uses to mark the end-of-
file.  Since the VAX also recognizes CTRL/Z as the EOF mark, this means the
receiving file will be closed automatically without any further attention.
If the file you are sending to the VAX is being generated by a program, the
operation of closing the OS/8 file will also 'close' the VAX file.  One can
also just send a CTRL/Z (octal 32, decimal 26) to close the VAX file without
closing the OS/8 file.  This allows the program to continue to use the VAX:
handler without the overhead of constantly re-loading it.

     This mode of operation is generally, but not always, convenient.  There
are times when it is desirable to merge several OS/8 files into one large VAX
file.  For example, one might like to create a temporary 'print file' and then
send over several PAL8 listings.  This won't work unless the 'no-EOF' switch
in the handler is set, as otherwise the CTRL/Z at the end of the first listing
will close the VAX file.  The state of this switch may be changed by altering
location '100' in the handler, either with a 'SET VAX: LOC 100=1' command, or
more easily by using the VAXX program:

		.R VAXX/C		(Set the 'concatenate' switch)
		.R VAXX/N		(Reset the switch to 'normal')

     A common use of the 'concatenated file' mode is to create a single VAX
file containing the OS/8 directory listings of many different floppy disks
or DECtapes.  These may then be conveniently printed all at once rather than
one-at-a-time.  To do this, run the TERMIN program and issue a $ CR DIR.LIS
command.  Then go back to OS/8 and proceed as follows:
		.R VAXX/C			(combine output)
		.DIR VAX:<RXA1:/E/P		(first floppy)
		.DIR VAX:<RXA1:/E/P		(second disk)
		.DIR VAX:<DTA0:/E/P		(first tape, etc.)
		.R VAXX/N			(reset EOF switch)
		.DIR VAX:<DTA1:/E/P		(do the last tape)
		.R TERMIN			(connect with VAX)
		$ PR/DEL DIR			(print directory file)
		(type Ctrl/E to return to OS/8)

     Once one has learned the few simple steps required to use this handler,
the procedures shown above will become almost second nature.  The most fre-
quent mistake is forgetting to initially create the file on the VAX.  If this
happens, the data sent by the handler is treated as a series of DCL commands!
This mistake can usually be detected by noting the much slower transfer rate
caused by all the error messages which are generated.


			Receiving files from the VAX

     The procedure for sending files from the host machine back to the PDP8 is
largely similar to that outlined for sending files the other way, with the ob-
vious switching of input/output operations.  One annoying difference on the
OS/8 side is that the .COPY command cannot be used - instead one must use the
.PUNCH command, due a deficiency in the FOTP program.  (PUNCH uses PIP rather
than FOTP.)  The basic outline of the steps required is as follows:

		1) Run the TERMIN program to login, etc.
		2) Issue a '$ TYPE filespec' command
		3) Type Ctrl/E to return to OS/8
		4) Call the VAX: handler as an input device

     Note especially that the $ TYPE command shown in Step 2 is but ONE method
for producing output from the VAX.  The procedure discussed below works with
ANY command which sends data to the terminal - thus the VAX: handler can be
used to 'capture' any sort of output, such as directory listings, mail, help
files, status reports, etc., and put this information in an OS/8 file.

     Since steps 3 & 4 typically take several seconds, it is obvious that un-
less something special is done in Step 2, most of the data sent to the PDP8
will be lost before the VAX: handler can be called.  There are two ways to
temporarily stop execution of the VAX command: the first is to type the com-
mand as shown, but WITHOUT hitting the 'return' key.  This simply leaves the
command in the input buffer, waiting for the user to say 'go ahead and do it'.
This is the recommended procedure.  The second method (useful in case habit
overcomes reason and you hit the RETURN key) is to QUICKLY type Ctrl/S several
times, which should stop output to the terminal.  The second method is not en-
tirely reliable since there appear to be times when the VAX simply ignores you.

     With the VAX in a suspended state there is now time to invoke the OS/8
utility which will use the VAX for input.  When the handler finally receives
an input request, it first sends a 'CTRL/Q' and then a 'RETURN', so no matter
how output was suspended, it will be sure to start.  Data received from the
VAX are then packed into the buffer area specified in the input request (us-
ing the standard OS/8 packing scheme) until either the buffer fills up or the
transmission ends.  If the buffer fills up first, the handler sends XOFF to
stop transmission until additional buffer space is available.  (Buffer space
is determined by the calling program, not by the handler).  When the VAX fin-
ally stops sending data the handler takes the 'EOF' exit.
     This raises an interesting question: how does the handler know when the
transmission is over?  The answer is: 'whenever no characters are received 
for a pre-determined period of time'.  Since the VAX does not send any 'EOF'
code to the terminal, this is the only way the handler can detect this con-
dition.  The 'pre-determined period of time' is an adjustable parameter in
the handler which must be set somewhat empirically, depending upon the re-
sponse time of a particular system.  The minimum delay is approximately .1
second, while delays of several seconds have been found necessary in cases
where the system is heavily loaded.  The 'delay time' may be set by changing
the value in location '300' of the handler, using either the 'SET LOC' com-
mand, or more conveniently, by means of the '/T' option in the VAXX program:

		.R VAXX/T=3		(set the delay to '3')
		.R VAXX/T=5		(increase it slightly)
		.R VAXX/T=0		(reset to the default value)

It is desirable in general to keep this value as small as possible so that
the handler will respond quickly to a 'real' EOF condition, while not 'tim-
ing out' too soon when the system is busy.

     Note that the VAX: handler may be used 'simultaneously' for both input
and output.  Thus the calling program may use this handler to send commands
to the VAX and then wait for the calculated results to come back before pro-
ceeding to the next step.  To check out the 'receive' side of the handler,
try sending a file to the VAX and then copying it back to the PDP8:

		.R TERMIN		(connect terminal to VAX)
		$ CR TESTFILE		(set up temporary output file)
		(Type CTRL/E to return to OS/8)
		.COPY VAX:<TEST.DA

		.R TERMIN		(get back on the VAX)
		$ TYPE TESTFILE.	(Remember: Don't type RETURN!)
		(Type CTRL/E to get back to OS/8)
		.PUNCH TEST.VX<VAX:	(Remember: Can't use .COPY!)
		.COMPAR TEST.DA,TEST.VX	(Check for any differences)

     As you will discover by actually trying this example, the two files are
ALMOST, but not EXACTLY, identical.  The difference is that the file received
from the VAX contains an extra blank line and a '$' at the end, which will be
recognized as the standard VAX 'prompt' line.  These spurious characters are
sent immediately after the data, and hence get appended to the file.  To avoid
this you can make a little command procedure that puts a 2-second 'wait' after
the 'TYPE' command, so the handler can 'time-out' before the command prompt:

		$ TYPE 'P1'		(list specified file on terminal)
		$ WAIT 0::2		(then wait for 2 seconds)

     If these two commands are put in a file called 'SEND8.COM', and you add
the definition $ SEND8:==@SEND8 to your login file, then you can use the com-
mand: '$ SEND8 filespec' in place of '$ TYPE filespec' to get an unblemished
version of the file.

     Here is another example: suppose you would like to keep a running record
of all the VAX time used to transfer files back and forth.  This information
is displayed by the 'LOGOUT' command, so you may proceed as follows:
		.R TERMIN		(time to end this session)
		$ LOGOUT/FULL		(But don't hit the RETURN key!)
		(Type CTRL/E to go back to OS/8)
		.PU VAXTIM<VAXTIM,VAX:

     The .PUNCH command adds the new accounting information to the end of the
cumulative VAXTIM file (which of course can be sent back to the VAX at some
later time for printing).

     As mentioned earlier, the VAX: handler can be used with practically any
OS/8 program, not just with 'file-transfer' programs like PIP and FOTP.  Here
is an example in which a VAX file is directly compared with an OS/8 file using
the SRCCOM program:

		.R TERMIN
		$ SEND8 filespec	(no RETURN, please)
		(Type CTRL/E to exit)
		.COMPARE original,VAX:	(do a direct comparison)
		first line of original
		first line of VAX file
		NO DIFFERENCES		(we hope!)
				Technical Details

     The key to successful communication between the PDP8 and other DEC oper-
ating systems lies in the use of the 'XON/XOFF' protocol.  According to this
scheme, the computer which is sending data must stop whenever it receives a
'XOFF' (DC3 or CTRL/S) character, resuming when it receives a 'XON' (DC1 or
CTRL/Q) code.  This allows the computer which is receiving data to stop the
transmission whenever it runs out of room in its input buffer.

     Aside from implementing this protocol, however, the user should be aware
that this handler also contains a number of other features tailored to the
'peculiarities' of the particular operating system it is inteneded to be used
with.  Here is a list of some of those special features:

     1) Linefeeds and nulls are suppressed during output.  The VAX treats
both of these as 'carriage returns' which creates spurious blank lines.

     2) CTRL/Z (the OS/8 end-of-file character) is normally sent to the VAX
in order to close the file.  This limits COPY operations to one-file-at-a-
time.  The user may, however, set the 'no-EOF' switch so that several OS/8
files can be merged into one VAX file.

     3) The handler uses a time-out loop to detect the end-of-transmission
from the VAX.  The response time of this loop can be adjusted to accomodate
different processors and/or different system activity levels.

     4) The handler sends 'XON+RETURN' when it is ready to receive data from
the VAX.  It then waits for a 'LINEFEED' plus a 'RETURN' before storing data
in the buffer.  These characters are not part of the data file, but are gen-
erated in response to the initial 'return'.

     5) Since the VAX does not immediately stop sending when it receives XOFF,
the handler stores up to 8 'overflow' characters in an internal buffer.  These
characters are then put in the user's input buffer the next time the handler is
called.  This feature limits usage of the handler with OS/8 FORTRAN IV programs
to situations in which no other OS/8 handlers are active at the same time, with
the exception of operations involving only the system device.

     6) Multiple 'linefeeds' during input are converted to multiple 'CR,LF'
pairs.  This solves a problem with early versions of VMS in which consecutive
blank lines (= multiple CR's in the OS/8 world) were sent as a single CR fol-
lowed by multiple LF's.  Since most OS/8 programs discard LF, the result was
the loss of all blank lines.

     7) Current versions of VMS seem to send multiple CR's.  For example, the
response to a DCL command is 'CR,LF,CR', with no apparent way to avoid the
second return.  Be sure to set both the /CRFILL and /LFFILL parameters to '0'
to avoid other spurious 'returns'.  You should also set the /FORM option so
that formfeeds will be preserved.

